BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:est-nord-est-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261108
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261109
SUMMARY:Deadline: Est-Nord-Est Artist Residency 2027-2028 (Saint-Jean-Port-
 Joli\, Quebec)
DESCRIPTION:Est-Nord-Est offers eight-week residencies in Saint-Jean-Port-J
 oli\, Quebec\, welcoming professional visual artists\, whether established
  or emerging\, from Quebec\, Canada and other countries\, with four artist
 s per season. FUNDING: a CAD 2\,080 residency honorarium\, an individual s
 tudio-workshop that also serves as lodging\, access to specialized equipme
 nt for wood\, metal and ceramics\, a documentation center\, technical\, ar
 tistic and logistical support\, and seasonal bicycle access. Materials\, t
 ravel and additional living expenses are the resident's responsibility. SE
 SSIONS: Spring (19 April - 11 June 2027)\, Summer (5 July - 27 August 2027
 \; a curated session with priority consideration for artists of African de
 scent)\, Fall (27 September - 19 November 2027) and Winter (24 January - 1
 8 March 2028). APPLY online with a CV (max 2 pages)\, artist statement and
  research intentions (max 1 page)\, and a portfolio of 10-15 images with d
 escriptions\; virtual mentoring sessions and alternative submission format
 s available on request. DEADLINE: 8 November 2026\, 11:59 PM.\n\nOrganisat
 ion: Est-Nord-Est\, residence d'artistes\nLocation: Saint-Jean-Port-Joli\,
  Quebec\, Canada. Open to professional visual artists\, established or eme
 rging\, from Quebec\, Canada and other countries\; materials\, travel and 
 additional living expenses are the resident's own responsibility.\nAward: 
 CAD 2\,080 residency honorarium\, plus a free individual studio-workshop w
 ith lodging\, access to specialized wood\, metal and ceramics equipment\, 
 a documentation center\, and technical\, artistic and logistical support. 
 Four artists per session. No application fee.\nDuration: Eight weeks. Sess
 ions: Spring 19 April - 11 June 2027\; Summer 5 July - 27 August 2027 (cur
 ated\, priority to artists of African descent)\; Fall 27 September - 19 No
 vember 2027\; Winter 24 January - 18 March 2028.\n\nMore: https://estnorde
 st.org/en/artist-residency/artist-residency-regular/?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://estnordest.org/en/artist-residency/artist-residency-regular/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Saint-Jean-Port-Joli\, Quebec\, Canada. Open to professional visua
 l artists\, established or emerging\, from Quebec\, Canada and other count
 ries\; materials\, travel and additional living expenses are the resident'
 s own responsibility.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:briarpatch-ai-resistance-winter-2027-pitches@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260918
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260919
SUMMARY:Deadline: Briarpatch Magazine: AI Resistance Issue\, Winter 2027 Ca
 ll for Pitches
DESCRIPTION:Briarpatch\, the Canadian magazine of grassroots politics\, ded
 icates its Winter 2027 issue to AI Resistance and is paying for articles\,
  investigative reporting\, historical analysis\, photo essays\, timelines\
 , profiles\, reading lists and art. SUGGESTED ANGLES include Indigenous-le
 d and local resistance to data centres\, exploitation and outsourcing of l
 abour in LLM training\, AI's impact on work and union resistance\, data mi
 ning and data sovereignty\, environmental and climate impacts\, artists an
 d AI (crisis of creativity and ownership)\, the language and inevitability
  narratives of AI\, policing and the surveillance state\, AI and disinform
 ation\, and investigations into Big Tech lobbying and media influence in C
 anada. PITCH: outline what ground your contribution covers\, estimated wor
 d count\, and your relevant experience\; first-time Briarpatch writers sho
 uld add a brief writing sample. Send to pitch [at] briarpatchmagazine.com.
  Accepted first drafts are due 14 October 2026 with collaborative editing 
 until late November\; publication is not guaranteed. No pitch fee. Deadlin
 e 18 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Briarpatch Magazine\nLocation: Regin
 a\, Canada (remote pitching\; Canadian focus)\nAward: CAD 150 for profiles
 \, short essays\, book reviews and parting shots (under ~1\,500 words)\; C
 AD 250 for feature stories and photo essays\; CAD 350 for research-based a
 rticles and investigative reporting (2\,000-2\,500 words). No pitch fee.\n
 Duration: First drafts due 14 October 2026\, editing through late November
 \n\nMore: https://briarpatchmagazine.com/winter-2027-pitches?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://briarpatchmagazine.com/winter-2027-pitches?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Regina\, Canada (remote pitching\; Canadian focus)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:vimeo-short-film-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115742Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261016
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261017
SUMMARY:Deadline: Vimeo Short Film Grant (USD 60\,000 for 5 filmmakers)
DESCRIPTION:Vimeo's Short Film Grant awards five emerging filmmakers USD 60
 \,000 in cash each to produce a short film\, alongside mentorship from cel
 ebrated filmmakers\, a loaned Panavision camera package\, OWC storage hard
 ware\, a one-year Vimeo Advanced subscription\, and distribution on Vimeo 
 with in-person screenings. ELIGIBILITY: residents of the United States or 
 Canada\, at least 18 years old\, who are emerging filmmakers having direct
 ed at least two short films but no feature films\; a valid Vimeo account i
 s required. TIMELINE: winners announced by 15 March 2027\; production must
  be completed by September 2027 and post-production by March 2028. HOW TO 
 APPLY: via SlideRoom with project details\, an artist statement\, team bio
 s\, creative materials (screenplay or treatment)\, a budget proposal\, a p
 roduction timeline and mentorship goals. No fee to apply. DEADLINE: 16 Oct
 ober 2026 at 11:59 PM ET (opened 21 September 2026).\n\nOrganisation: Vime
 o\nLocation: Online application. Open to residents of the United States an
 d Canada.\nAward: Five filmmakers each receive USD 60\,000 in cash for pro
 duction\, plus a one-year Vimeo Advanced subscription\, mentorship from ce
 lebrated filmmakers\, a loaned Panavision camera package\, OWC storage har
 dware\, and distribution on Vimeo with in-person screenings. Free to apply
 .\nDuration: Production must be completed by September 2027 and post-produ
 ction by March 2028\; winners announced by 15 March 2027\n\nMore: https://
 vimeo.com/shortfilmgrant?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
URL:https://vimeo.com/shortfilmgrant?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online application. Open to residents of the United States and Can
 ada.
END:VEVENT
